{
  "id": "cross-modality-matching",
  "term": "cross-modality matching",
  "definition": "Cross-modality matching is a psychophysical method in which a person adjusts one kind of sensory or physical dimension to match the perceived magnitude of another, such as changing line length to represent how loud a sound seems. It is used to track subjective intensity over time, though it can show systematic bias and is often checked by comparing it with the reverse task, magnitude production.",
